.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs programs written in Wattle,
a Lisp-like programming language.
Wattle reimplements the virtual machine,
compiler and core library from the Janet programming language
in Zig with a syntax inspired by Clojure.
With no
.Ar script ,
and no option that does work of its own,
.Nm
starts a REPL (read-eval-print loop).
.Pp
Options are processed in order,
so an option applies to the options and the
.Ar script
that follow it.
The first argument that does not begin with
.Ql "-"
is the
.Ar script ,
and every argument after it is passed to the script.
Once the script has run,
.Nm
calls the
.Ql "main"
function it defines,
if any,
with the script’s path followed by
.Ar args .
The same list is bound to
.Ql "*args*" .
.Pp
.Nm
exits when the script and any tasks it scheduled on the event
loop have finished.
.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Fl h ,
.Fl "-help"
.Xc
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Xo
.Fl v ,
.Fl "-version"
.Xc
Print the version of Wattle and exit.
.It Xo
.Fl e ,
.Fl "-eval"
.Ar code
.Xc
Evaluate
.Ar code
as Wattle source.
The REPL is not started afterwards unless
.Fl r
is also given.
.It Xo
.Fl E ,
.Fl "-expression"
.Ar code
.Oo
.Ar arguments
.No ...
.Oc
.Xc
Evaluate
.Ar code
as the body of a
.Ql "short-fn"
and call it with every remaining argument as a string.
No option after
.Ar code
is processed.
For example,
.Ql "wattle -E \(aq\*(lpprint $0\*(rp\(aq 12"
prints
.Ql "12" .
.It Xo
.Fl l ,
.Fl "-library"
.Ar module
.Xc
Import
.Ar module
into the environment before continuing,
without a prefix,
so its bindings are available to later options,
the
.Ar script
and the REPL.
.It Xo
.Fl m ,
.Fl "-syspath"
.Ar path
.Xc
Set
.Ql "*syspath*" ,
the directory system modules are loaded from,
to
.Ar path .
This replaces the value taken from
.Ev WATTLE_PATH .
.It Xo
.Fl i ,
.Fl "-image"
.Xc
Treat
.Ar script
as an image made by
.Fl c
rather than as source,
load it and call its
.Ql "main"
function.
.It Xo
.Fl c ,
.Fl "-compile"
.Ar source
.Oo
.Ar output
.Oc
.Xc
Load the module at
.Ar source
and write an image of its environment to
.Ar output .
If
.Ar output
is omitted,
it is
.Ar source
with
.Ql "\&.wattle"
replaced by
.Ql "\&.jimage" .
.It Xo
.Fl k ,
.Fl "-flycheck"
.Xc
Compile
.Ar script
without running it and report any errors.
With no
.Ar script ,
check standard input instead.
Errors do not change the exit status.
.It Xo
.Fl p ,
.Fl "-persistent"
.Xc
Do not exit from inside the loader when a script or a module
loaded with
.Fl l
raises a top-level error.
The error propagates instead,
and the process still exits with status 1.
.It Xo
.Fl r ,
.Fl "-repl"
.Xc
Start the REPL after the
.Ar script
or the other options have run.
.It Xo
.Fl s ,
.Fl "-stdin"
.Xc
Read REPL input as raw lines from standard input and write prompts
to standard output.
Implies
.Fl r .
.It Xo
.Fl q ,
.Fl "-quiet"
.Xc
Do not print the banner when the REPL starts.
.It Xo
.Fl R ,
.Fl "-noprofile"
.Xc
Do not load the profile named by
.Ev WATTLE_PROFILE .
.It Xo
.Fl d ,
.Fl "-debug"
.Xc
Set
.Ql "*debug*"
and
.Ql "*redef*" .
An error in the REPL enters the debugger,
and a top-level definition can be rebound.
.It Xo
.Fl n ,
.Fl "-nocolor"
.Xc
Print values,
errors and documentation without ANSI colour.
.It Xo
.Fl N ,
.Fl "-color"
.Xc
Print values,
errors and documentation with ANSI colour.
.It Xo
.Fl w ,
.Fl "-lint-warn"
.Ar level
.Xc
Set the lint level at which a message is printed as a warning.
.Ar level
is one of
.Ql "none" ,
.Ql "relaxed" ,
.Ql "normal" ,
.Ql "strict"
or
.Ql "all" ,
written without a leading colon,
or a number.
The default is
.Ql "normal" .
.It Xo
.Fl x ,
.Fl "-lint-error"
.Ar level
.Xc
Set the lint level at which a message stops compilation with
an error.
.Ar level
takes the same values as for
.Fl w .
The default is
.Ql "none" .
.It Xo
.Fl b ,
.Fl "-install"
.Ar directory
.Xc
Install the bundle in
.Ar directory .
.It Xo
.Fl B ,
.Fl "-reinstall"
.Ar name
.Xc
Reinstall the installed bundle
.Ar name .
.It Xo
.Fl u ,
.Fl "-uninstall"
.Ar name
.Xc
Uninstall the bundle
.Ar name .
.It Xo
.Fl U ,
.Fl "-update-all"
.Xc
Reinstall every installed bundle.
.It Xo
.Fl P ,
.Fl "-prune"
.Xc
Uninstall every orphaned bundle:
one marked for automatic removal that no other bundle depends
on.
.It Xo
.Fl L ,
.Fl "-list"
.Xc
List the installed bundles.
.It Xo
.Fl -
.Xc
Stop processing options.
The next argument is the
.Ar script
even if it begins with
.Ql "-" .
.El
.Pp
An unrecognised option prints the usage summary and exits with
status 0.
.
.Sh REPL
The REPL reads a line at a time and evaluates each form once
it is complete.
The prompt shows the line number and any open delimiters.
Documentation for a binding is shown with
.Ql "\*(lpdoc symbol\*(rp" ,
and
.Ql "\*(lpdoc\*(rp"
lists the bindings in scope.
.Pp
Prompts are written to standard error,
or to standard output with
.Fl s .
.Pp
Before the first prompt,
the REPL loads the file named by
.Ev WATTLE_PROFILE
into its environment,
unless
.Fl R
was given.
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ev WATTLE_PATH
.Xc
A list of directories to load system modules from,
separated by
.Ql "\&:" ,
or by
.Ql "\&;"
on Windows.
The last directory becomes
.Ql "*syspath*"
and is searched first.
If unset,
.Ql "*syspath*"
is
.Eo
.Pa /usr/local/lib/wattle
.Ec .
.It Xo
.Ev WATTLE_PROFILE
.Xc
A Wattle file to load before the REPL starts.
It is not loaded when a
.Ar script
runs without
.Fl r .
.It Xo
.Ev WATTLE_HISTFILE
.Xc
A file to keep the REPL’s history in.
Each form submitted at the REPL on a terminal is appended to
it,
and the forms in it are recalled with the Up key in a later
session.
If unset,
history lasts only until the REPL exits.
.It Xo
.Ev NO_COLOR
.Xc
If set to a non-empty value,
disable colour.
Colour is otherwise used when both standard output and standard
error are terminals.
.El
.Pp
An environment variable set to the empty string is treated as
unset.
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits
.Ql "0"
on success and
.Ql "1"
if a script or an option raises an error that is not handled.
An error in the REPL is printed and does not end the session.
A program can choose its own status with
.Ql "\*(lpos/exit status\*(rp" .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start the REPL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle
.Ed
.Pp
Run a script with two arguments:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle hello\&.wattle Alice Bob
.Ed
.Pp
Evaluate an expression and print its result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle -e \(aq\*(lpprint \*(lp+ 1 2 3\*(rp\*(rp\(aq
.Ed
.Pp
Compile a program into an image and run the image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle -c hello\&.wattle hello\&.jimage
$ wattle -i hello\&.jimage Alice
.Ed
.Pp
Check a script for compile errors without running it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle -k hello\&.wattle
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Wattle does not yet have a written manual.
The core library documents itself:
.Ql "\*(lpdoc symbol\*(rp"
prints a binding’s documentation in the REPL and
.Ql "\*(lpdoc\*(rp"
lists the bindings in scope.
Example programs are distributed with the source at
.Lk https://github.com/pyrmont/wattle .
.
.Sh HISTORY
Wattle began as a fork of Janet,
the language created by Calvin Rose.
It has its own version numbering:
the binding
.Ql "wattle/version"
reports that version.
